  "live_records": {
    "q-36624": {
      "dom_id": "step-q36624",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given that $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}),$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ exists.",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2} & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2} & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
