    "q-72000": {
      "dom_id": "step-q72000",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z),$ find the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}$ exists.",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{z}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $z$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
