    "q-132751": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132751",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{z+y-{x}^{2}}?$\n\nI. The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(0,0,1)$\n\nII. ${f}_{y}(1,2,0)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{z}(1,1,0)$ is defined",
      "worked_solution": "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{z+y-{x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z+y-{x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z+y-{x}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,0,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,0,1)=\\sqrt{1+0-{0}^{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(0,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,0,1) & =-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{1+0-{0}^{2}}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,2,0)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,2,0)=\\sqrt{0+2-{1}^{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(1,2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,2,0) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{0+2-{1}^{2}}}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(1,1,0)$ into ${f}_{z}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{z}(1,1,0)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{0+1-{1}^{2}}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only'.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
